\description{
Print, summarize and extract the results of \code{\link[=rmedsem]{rmedsem()}}.
}
\section{Printing and summarizing}{

\code{print()} gives a detailed, step-by-step description of the results: a
table of the tests of the indirect effect (one column per estimation
method), the steps and conclusions of the Baron and Kenny and/or Zhao,
Lynch & Chen approaches, and the effect sizes. For \code{blavaan} models, the
table reports posterior summaries instead; for moderated mediation with
\code{modsem}, the moderation effects are printed in addition.

\code{summary()} collects the same results in compact form: a table of the
indirect (for each estimation method), direct and total effects, the type
of mediation, and the effect sizes.
}

\section{Extracting results}{

\code{coef()} returns the estimated indirect, direct and total effects,
\code{confint()} their confidence (or, for \code{blavaan} models, credible)
intervals, and \code{nobs()} the number of observations. \code{as.data.frame()}
returns the estimates of the indirect effect for all estimation methods.

The indirect effect is estimated with several methods (see
\code{object$est.methods}), which all give the same point estimate but
different standard errors and intervals. By default, \code{coef()} and
\code{confint()} use the method that also underlies the Zhao, Lynch & Chen
approach: \code{"montc"} (Monte-Carlo) for \code{lavaan} and \code{modsem}, \code{"boot"}
(bootstrap) for \code{cSEM} and \code{plssem}, and \code{"bayes"} for \code{blavaan}.
}

\section{Extending the printed output}{

\code{print.rmedsem()} handles all backends that provide the elements described
in section 'Adding a backend' of \code{\link[=rmedsem]{rmedsem()}}. Backends that need a
different output provide a method for their subclass, either replacing
the default output (\code{print.rmedsem_blavaan()}) or extending it with
\code{\link[=NextMethod]{NextMethod()}} (\code{print.rmedsem_modsem()}).
}

\examples{
mod.txt <- "
read ~ math
science ~ read + math
"
mod <- lavaan::sem(mod.txt, data = rmedsem::hsbdemo)
out <- rmedsem(mod, indep = "math", med = "read", dep = "science")

# detailed output
print(out)

# compact summary and its elements
s <- summary(out)
s
s$mediation
s$effects

# extract estimates
coef(out)
confint(out)
confint(out, parm = "indirect", method = "sobel")
nobs(out)
as.data.frame(out)

}
